  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 141 AM MDT Sat Aug 22 2026\n\nThe monsoon has once again arrived across the area, I believe \nthis is the third or fourth appearance so far. So far, it has \nbehaved. We do have some light showers across mainly the western\nhalf of the area but nothing heavy so far. The main concern in \nthe forecast will be the potential for heavy rain and possible \nstrong thunderstorms today. Right now, in this meteorologists \nopinion, potential is mixed for if we will see widespread of \neither.\n\nIn regards to heavy rain, the main factor will be the amount of \nmoisture in the atmosphere. Dewpoints have been slow to rise so\nfar, but they should this afternoon, rising into the 50s in \nportions of the Bighorn Basin as well as Johnson and Natrona \nCounties. Precipitable water values should also be rather high. \nThe GFS, for example, has precipitable water values about 175 to\n200 percent of normal. This had been overdone by around 15 to \n20 percent the past few events though. Even with this, \nprecipitable water values over 150 percent of normal look fairly\nlikely. There will also be some forcing in the form of a \nshortwave moving in from the west. This should enhance chances \nfor all areas, so everyone will have a chance. The best chance \nstill looks to be in northwestern Wyoming, generally a 1 in 2 \nchance, and there is decent model agreement in this. As for \nsevere thunderstorms, there looks to be a decent amount of \ninstability. This is especially so in the favored portions of \nour area, mainly east of the Bighorn Range in Johnson and \nNatrona Counties. Lifted indices fall as low as minus 4 to minus\n5 with close to 1500 J/kg of surface CAPE here. There is also a\ndecent amount of directional shear in this area. This, plus the\nample moisture, may increase the hail threat in this area. We \ndo have a Marginal risk of severe weather in portions of \nJohnson and Natrona Counties, and this could be expanded \nwestward with the 13Z update. All areas could have a \nthunderstorm but with less instability and shear most storms \nwould be sub-severe.\n\nHowever, there are some factors against possible hazards. In \nregards to flooding and thunderstorms, it is cloud cover. Much \nof the area, especially western Wyoming, is covered by clouds as\nI write this around midnight. If it stays mostly cloudy for \nmuch of the day, this could limit instability somewhat and \ndecrease the chance of stronger thunderstorms. There will likely\nbe some areas that will be sunnier though, especially further \neast. And there is one more mitigating factor for heavy rain and\npossible flooding. With the shortwave moving through, there \nwill be some steering flow today. It won't be strong, but most \nareas will have 15 to 25 knots at 500 millibars which should \nkeep storms moving somewhat. We continue to have a Marginal \nrisk of excessive rainfall, and persistent storms could still \ncause flooding, but it may decrease the chance somewhat. The \nshortwave should clear the area by midnight, but some showers \nwill likely linger through the night. As for temperatures, more \ncloud cover should lower them around 5 to 8 degrees, but it will\nstill be above normal for almost all areas.\n\nThe forecast from Sunday through much of next week looks fairly \nsimilar. We will remain on the edge of the ridge. Some monsoonal \nmoisture will stick around, with precipitable water values remaining \nabove average through much of the period. However, they do look \nlower than today, so the chance of convection should be somewhat \nlower.  Most guidance favors western Wyoming at this time. However, \ngiven the poor performance of the models in regards to timing and \nplacement of the showers and storms, confidence in the details \nremains low. Temperatures will also cool off somewhat, back down to \nnear to just slightly above normal levels for late August. There \ncontinues to be some indications of drier air moving in for the\nend of next week or next weekend, possibly shutting off the \nmonsoon and bring a period of warm, but not really hot weather \nfor the end of August.",
